{
 "version": 1,
 "title": "Advanced 1 \u00b7 Vibrato that fades in",
 "text": "Real players don't start a note with vibrato: it creeps in as the note holds. The trick is to modulate the amount of a modulation. The LFO doesn't go straight into the oscillator's FM: it goes through a VCA, and a slow envelope (ATTACK 0.8 s) opens that VCA. So when a key goes down the vibrato is shut, and over almost a second it opens up. Watch the VCA's output trace: the wobble grows. Any VCA in a modulation path is a depth control something else can turn.\n\nTry: the vibrato envelope's ATTACK (0 is vibrato at once, 2 s a slow bloom); the oscillator's FM (the width); move the envelope's cable off the vibrato VCA's CV and plug MIDI In's MOD WHEEL in instead: now the wheel brings the vibrato in, as on most synths; then PRESSURE, if your keyboard sends it.",
 "voices": 4,
